Snort 3 IDS Flow Visualizer
Real-Time Network Traffic Analysis & Threat Intelligence Integration
📌 Project Overview

This project transforms raw Snort 3 Intrusion Detection System (IDS) logs into a high-visibility security dashboard. By leveraging the ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Filebeat) and Grafana, I created a visualization pipeline that allows security analysts to identify "top-talker" attacker IPs and perform instant threat reputation lookups.
🛠️ Tech Stack

    IDS: Snort 3

    Security OS: Kali Purple

    Data Pipeline: Filebeat & Elasticsearch

    Visualization: Grafana (Sankey Panel Plugin)

    Threat Intel: VirusTotal API Integration

🚀 Key Features

    Sankey Traffic Flow: Visualizes the volume of traffic between Origin / Attacker IPs and Target / Asset IPs, making it easy to spot scanning activity or potential lateral movement.

    Advanced Data Transformations: Utilized Grafana binary operations to merge disparate data frames (Source/Destination counts) into a unified traffic metric.

    Click-to-Investigate: Integrated custom Data Links that allow users to click any IP in the dashboard to launch an immediate reputation check on VirusTotal.

    Threshold-Based Alerting: Nodes change color (Green to Red) based on traffic volume, reducing "log fatigue" for analysts.

📂 Repository Contents

    /dashboards: Contains the snort-flow-visualizer.json export. You can import this into your own Grafana instance to replicate the setup.

    /screenshots: Visual proof of the live dashboard and threat intelligence links.

    /configs: Example filebeat.yml and Snort 3 configuration snippets used for this pipeline.

💡 Engineering Challenges

    Schema Auditing: Successfully audited Elasticsearch index mappings to resolve "Field Not Found" errors by identifying the correct JSON paths for IP metadata.

    Logic Merging: Overcame limitations in single-query frames by implementing a transformation sequence: Join by field -> Add field from calculation -> Organize fields.

🤖 AI Collaboration

This project workflow was optimized using Google Gemini as a technical co-pilot for debugging complex Grafana transformations, auditing nested JSON structures, and architecting the data pipeline documentation.

